ntdll: exec child loaders through the system linker on Android.

Starting a session works: the app builds an argv of
{ /system/bin/linker64, <wine>/bin/wine, ... } and execs that, because an app at
targetSdk >= 29 may not execve a file in its own data directory but may exec the
system linker and hand it the program.

Every *child* Windows process skips that. Wine creates them by re-execing its
own loader from preloader_exec(), which runs

    execv( argv[0], argv );          /* <loader>-preloader */
    execv( argv[1], argv + 1 );      /* the loader itself */

Both binaries live in the app's data directory, so both are refused, and the
symptom is lopsided in a way that hides the cause: the first process runs
perfectly and anything that spawns a second one -- an installer, a launcher stub,
most non-trivial applications -- dies with EACCES.

The argv the caller builds is already the right shape. exec_wineloader()
documents that "argv[0] and argv[1] must be reserved for the preloader and
loader respectively", so writing the linker path into argv[0] yields exactly
{ linker, loader, args... }. The linker consumes its own argv[0] before handing
control over, so the loader still sees itself at argv[0] and Wine's argv[0]
dispatch -- which is what makes one binary serve as wineboot, winecfg and the
rest -- is unaffected.

The preloader is skipped rather than redirected. It exists to reserve low
address space before the dynamic linker claims it, so running it *under* the
linker would defeat its purpose. Wine's own reserved-area fallback covered the
64-bit case in every run observed so far (images were placed in a reserved area
with no preloader in the process at all). The 32-bit/WoW64 path depends on that
reservation more heavily and is the likeliest place for this shortcut to surface
as an address-space failure; it has not been exercised yet.

Not suitable upstream as-is: the linker path is hardcoded, and a real fix would
come from configure or an environment variable rather than __ANDROID__.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
@@ -437,6 +437,35 @@
 
 static void preloader_exec( char **argv )
 {
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+    /* Exec the system linker, never the loader directly.
+     *
+     * An app at targetSdk >= 29 may not execve a file in its own data
+     * directory, and both binaries this function would otherwise run live
+     * there: the preloader at lib/wine/<arch>-unix/wine-preloader and the
+     * loader beside it. Android permits execing /system/bin/linker64 and
+     * passing the program as its first argument, which is how the session is
+     * started in the first place -- but every *child* Windows process comes
+     * through here instead, so without this an installer that spawns a second
+     * process fails with EACCES while the first one runs fine.
+     *
+     * argv is already shaped for this: the caller reserves argv[0] for the
+     * preloader and puts the loader in argv[1], so overwriting argv[0] with
+     * the linker path gives exactly { linker, loader, args... }. The linker
+     * consumes its own argv[0], so the loader still sees itself at argv[0]
+     * and Wine's argv[0] dispatch keeps working.
+     *
+     * The preloader is skipped, not redirected. Its job is to reserve low
+     * address space before the dynamic linker claims it, and it cannot run
+     * under the linker because the linker is what it exists to preempt.
+     * Wine's own reserved-area fallback covers the 64-bit case (observed
+     * placing images in a reserved area with no preloader present); the
+     * 32-bit/WoW64 case leans on it harder and is the more likely place for
+     * this to show up as an address-space failure.
+     */
+    argv[0] = (char *)"/system/bin/linker64";
+    execv( argv[0], argv );
+#else
 #ifdef HAVE_WINE_PRELOADER
     asprintf( &argv[0], "%s-preloader", argv[1] );
 #ifdef __APPLE__
@@ -452,6 +481,7 @@
     free( argv[0] );
 #endif
     execv( argv[1], argv + 1 );
+#endif
 }
 
 /* exec the appropriate wine loader for the specified machine */
